(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-10"] = {"8.1":{"s":"A function is continuous at a point a if its graph has no break there. Three things must hold: f(a) is defined, the limit of f(x) as x approaches a exists, and the limit equals f(a). If any of these fails, the function is discontinuous at a. A jump discontinuity has different left and right limits. An infinite discontinuity has a limit that grows without bound.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> a) f(x) = f(a)","n":"Third condition of continuity at a. It needs f(a) defined and the limit to exist.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.175"}],"w":["All three conditions are needed: a limit that exists is not enough if f(a) is undefined or has another value.","Check the left and right limits separately at a point where the rule changes."]},"8.2":{"s":"On an open interval (a, b), continuity means no break at any point between a and b. On a closed interval [a, b] we need that, and also continuity from the right at a and from the left at b. Polynomials are continuous everywhere, and the sum, difference and product of continuous functions is continuous. A quotient is continuous wherever the denominator is not zero.","f":[{"x":"lim(x -> a^(+)) f(x) = f(a)","n":"Continuity from the right at a. From the left at b: lim(x -> b^(-)) f(x) = f(b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 2, p.175"}],"w":["On a closed interval the end points need only one-sided continuity.","A quotient is discontinuous at the zeros of its denominator."]},"8.3":{"s":"The intermediate value theorem says: if f is continuous on [a, b] and k is any number between f(a) and f(b), then f takes the value k at some point c between a and b. A special case: if f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs, the equation f(x) = 0 has a root between a and b. It tells us only that a root exists, not what it is.","w":["The function must be continuous on the whole interval; with a jump the theorem can fail.","It proves that a root exists in (a, b); it does not give the root or say how many there are."]}};
